    "features": 39,
    "folds": 4,
    "limitation": "This study was run before the full-archive training run finished. Two pairs give every bar exactly one binary choice, so every rate here is measured on the thinnest possible universe, the series is constructed rather than real, and effective_bars shows how few independent outcomes stand behind each rate. It is a demonstration that the table can be produced and read, not evidence anybody should rule on. Re-run it over the full archive's out-of-sample file and dataset before the ruling: python -m acsoe.research.training --ranking-study data/derived/oos_<run_id>.parquet --dataset data/derived/dataset_<stamp>.parquet",
    "measured_on": "2026-09-13",
    "method": "acsoe.research.ranking_study.build_report over one training run's out-of-sample file joined to its dataset: on every decision bar carrying two or more pairs, the pair ranked first by each feature in each direction is taken, and its realised label is counted. The choice is engine 7's `rank_universe` rule: a non-finite value (null, NaN, inf) is no value and sorts after every valued pair in both directions, and ties and valueless pairs go in pair-name order",
    "note": "No feature is recommended here and the rows are in feature order, not in outcome order: an ordering by outcome is a recommendation with the word left off. Spec 75 \u2014 the operator rules.",
